/*
  Arivu — iOS copy catalogue.

  One catalogue, one text (leaves/design.md §6). Keys are the SAME KEYS as
  android/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml, so translation later is a data change and a parity
  check can compare the two files key by key (Design's W68, tools/check_copy.py).

  A string diverges from Android ONLY where the platform noun or the platform fact differs — the
  object the user touches (Home Screen, Mail, the App Store), or a capability that exists on one OS
  and not the other. Every divergence below is one of the rows in design.md §6 and says so.
  Tone, structure and claims never diverge.

  Copy rules (leaves/design.md §6):
  - Plain words, short sentences. Many readers use English as a second language.
  - One idea per string. Never build a sentence by joining strings in code; use %1$@ / %1$ld.
  - Name what the user can do next. No jargon ("tokens", "context", "jetsam").
  - British spelling, kept consistent. Translation is decision D-014.
  - Typographic apostrophes and quotes (’ “ ”).
  - "This phone", not "iPhone", except where the user must do something iPhone-shaped (D-044).
  - a11y_* strings are read by VoiceOver only.

  SOURCE: leaves/design/copy.md (Design, spine 0.2). This file is a RENDERING of it, not a source.
  Two mechanical differences from the catalogue, both required by the platform and neither a change
  of text:
    - placeholders: the catalogue writes `%1$s` (Java/Kotlin); iOS writes `%1$@` for a string and
      `%1$ld` for an integer. Same position, same meaning. tools/check_copy.py must normalise these.
    - `back`, `incompatible_uninstall`, `gate_abi`, `gate_low_ram`, `notification_channel` and
      `notification_writing` are marked `platforms: [android]` in the catalogue and are absent here.

  The `privacy_*` bodies are owned by COMPLIANCE (D-022, W43). The catalogue records only how the iOS
  text must differ, not the words. What is below is Engineering's rendering of those change notes so
  the app is readable offline today; Compliance's final text replaces it, in both platform renderings
  at once. D-038 settles the claim in `about_private_body` and `privacy_internet_body`.
*/

